    "body": "The EBANKU Nail Polish Protector has a regret score of 81/100 on Pregret, meaning most people who buy it end up wishing they hadn't. At first glance, the premise sounds practical: 140 pieces of peel-off sticker tape designed to protect skin around nails during polish application and stamping. In reality, owners report the product falls short of its core function in multiple ways that undermine the entire purchase.\n\n## The Real Reasons\n\n### Stickers Don't Adhere Properly to Skin\n\nThe most common complaint centers on adhesion. The U-shaped tape is designed to stick to skin around the nail bed, but owners report the stickers frequently slip, curl, or fall off during application. This defeats the primary purpose—protecting skin from polish drips and stamp ink. Many users find themselves holding the stickers in place manually, which makes the product pointless and adds frustration to an already detailed process.\n\n### Removal Causes Skin Irritation\n\nWhile the stickers are advertised as \"soft plastic,\" removing them often leaves behind redness, minor irritation, or residual adhesive residue on the skin. Some users report having to wash multiple times to remove sticky buildup. For people with sensitive skin, the removal process can be uncomfortable enough to outweigh any time-saving benefit the stickers might have offered.\n\n### Size and Shape Don't Match Varied Nail Shapes\n\nNails come in different widths and shapes, but the U-shaped design is one-size-fits-most. Owners with wider nail beds or unusually shaped nails find the stickers either too small, too large, or awkwardly positioned. This means the tape doesn't provide complete coverage where intended, leaving gaps where polish can still contact skin.\n\n### Excessive Waste with Low Actual Usability\n\nThe 140-piece count sounds generous until you start using them. Many users report that 30 to 50 percent of the stickers in each pack are either unusable due to manufacturing defects, already damaged from packaging, or simply don't stick to skin regardless of technique. This means the effective count is closer to 70 usable pieces, making the actual value lower than the initial appeal suggests.\n\n### Application Is More Time-Consuming Than Advertised\n\nThe stickers are meant to save time, but owners report the opposite. Applying 140 separate pieces (or portions of them) to ten fingers takes longer than simply being careful with a polish brush or using painter's tape—the traditional alternative. The process of alignment, smoothing down edges, and repositioning stickers that don't stick properly adds minutes to nail prep.\n\n### Quality Inconsistency Across Batches\n\nSeveral purchasers note that batches vary in stickiness and durability. Some packs arrive with adequately adhesive tape; others are barely sticky from the start. There's no way to assess quality before opening, so you might invest in a pack only to find the tape won't stick at all on your first use.\n\n## Warning Signs Before You Buy\n\nAsk yourself these questions before ordering:\n\n- Do you have sensitive skin or allergies to adhesives?\n- Are your nails wider than average or unusually shaped?\n- Do you do nail art frequently enough to justify a 140-piece supply?\n- Are you comfortable with products that require multiple attempts to work as intended?\n- Do you have time to apply and adjust dozens of small stickers before each manicure?\n- Have you used painter's tape or other nail protection methods with success?\n\nIf you answered yes to three or more of these questions, this product is likely not the solution for you.\n\n## What to Try Instead\n\nBefore committing to the EBANKU stickers, consider alternatives that reviewers frequently mention as more effective: traditional painter's tape (which adheres better and costs less), liquid masking tape formulas that peel away cleanly, or simply improving your brush control with practice and better-quality polish brushes. Some people find barrier balms applied around the nail perimeter more practical than stickers. Others accept minor cleanup as part of the nail art process.\n\n## US and Canada Buying Notes\n\nOn **Amazon.com**, the standard return window is 30 days. If you order EBANKU Nail Polish Protector and find the stickers don't adhere or work as expected, you have this window to request a return. Keep the packaging and document your experience if possible.\n\nOn **Amazon.ca**, the return policy is similarly 30 days for most items, though some sellers may have extended windows. Check the product listing's return policy before purchasing, as third-party sellers can set their own terms.\n\nGiven the regret score and the consensus about performance issues, consider starting with a smaller nail protection product or alternative method before committing to a large multi-pack."
